# Plain commit messages

The subject says what the commit does in one plain clause, the way you would say it to a teammate. Simplify it even at the cost of precision; the diff holds the detail. Do not stack qualifiers, smuggle in the reason, or list the things touched.

The body is optional and usually absent. Code is its own language, so never narrate what the diff shows: no file lists, no "X now does Y", no "Z stays as it is". Add a body only for a why the code cannot show, or a how a reader might otherwise undo. One short paragraph is the norm, two is the ceiling.

`writing-git-commits` has the full guidance and examples.
